          "title": "You'll want to know: فردای آن روز",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "فردای آن روز (fardâ-ye ân ruz) — \"the next day, the following day\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "فردای آن روز (fardâ-ye ân ruz) دوباره به بیمارستان رفتیم. Fardâ-ye ân ruz dobâre be bimârestân raftim. The next day we went to the hospital again."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In a story set in the past, fardâ alone would mean \"tomorrow\"; this phrase, or ruz-e ba'd, gives \"the day after\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "fardâ \"tomorrow\", ân \"that\" and ruz \"day\" are inherited Persian; EtymDB takes ruz back through Middle Persian to Old Persian rauča-, \"day\"."
            }
          ]
        },
